WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.425
This is a decision briefing for a CIO,
a budget officer and an endpoint lead.

2
00:00:05.425 --> 00:00:08.427
An agency weighing a
refresh is being asked to

3
00:00:08.427 --> 00:00:11.587
fund a distribution point
at every field office.

4
00:00:11.587 --> 00:00:15.221
The question is whether that is
the right way to deliver content,

5
00:00:15.221 --> 00:00:18.375
or whether fewer servers and
a better network do the job.

6
00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:22.448
The numbers come from the worked case
in the Network and Transport book,

7
00:00:22.448 --> 00:00:26.175
a framing pattern with every cost
labeled as a planning estimate.

8
00:00:26.175 --> 00:00:30.025
We'll cover the decision, the
questions to answer first,

9
00:00:30.025 --> 00:00:32.600
the options, the plan, and the ask.

10
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.432
The request is about fifty
million dollars to refresh

11
00:00:39.432 --> 00:00:43.337
distribution point hardware across
about fifteen hundred offices.

12
00:00:43.337 --> 00:00:46.123
That's thirty-three thousand
dollars per office,

13
00:00:46.123 --> 00:00:50.649
nearly twice the cost of a fully loaded
server at the program's planning prices.

14
00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:54.054
Configuration Manager's own
limits say the estate needs

15
00:00:54.054 --> 00:00:57.675
about twenty-four distribution
points, not fifteen hundred.

16
00:00:57.675 --> 00:01:00.227
So the position is
simple: defer the refresh,

17
00:01:00.227 --> 00:01:04.409
pending a network modernization
assessment, because the same problem

18
00:01:04.409 --> 00:01:08.237
can be solved for about ten million
dollars in first-year capital.

19
00:01:08.237 --> 00:01:12.500
The next slide lists what has to be
answered before any money moves.

20
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:17.988
Five questions come first.

21
00:01:17.988 --> 00:01:22.496
Is the fifty million an equipment price
or five-year total cost of ownership,

22
00:01:22.496 --> 00:01:25.501
and what is bundled beyond
the distribution point?

23
00:01:25.501 --> 00:01:28.145
What will the distribution
point count be when

24
00:01:28.145 --> 00:01:30.925
co-management completes,
and in which fiscal year?

25
00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:33.188
What does each office's
circuit cost today?

26
00:01:33.188 --> 00:01:37.025
That one number decides whether
the alternative pays for itself.

27
00:01:37.025 --> 00:01:40.950
How many managed endpoints are there,
since that sets the server count?

28
00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:45.350
And do branch security rules allow
devices to share content with each other?

29
00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.280
Each answer comes from records
the agency already holds.

30
00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:57.955
Microsoft's size and scale page sets four
thousand clients per distribution point.

31
00:01:57.955 --> 00:02:01.243
Seventy thousand endpoints
need eighteen at the ceiling,

32
00:02:01.243 --> 00:02:02.955
and twenty-four with headroom.

33
00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:07.483
The other fourteen hundred seventy-six
compensate for slow branch circuits,

34
00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:09.817
and content has since moved to the cloud.

35
00:02:09.817 --> 00:02:11.117
Timing matters too.

36
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:14.887
Microsoft has moved Configuration
Manager to an annual release

37
00:02:14.887 --> 00:02:18.517
cadence and says new device
management work happens in Intune.

38
00:02:18.517 --> 00:02:22.947
Hardware bought this fiscal year
depreciates through fiscal 2031,

39
00:02:22.947 --> 00:02:26.031
so a refresh can outlive
the platform it serves.

40
00:02:26.031 --> 00:02:27.617
The role isn't obsolete.

41
00:02:27.617 --> 00:02:30.670
The distribution point at every office is.

42
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.307
Three options sit on one line.

43
00:02:36.307 --> 00:02:40.808
Refresh keeps fifteen hundred
servers, about fifty million dollars,

44
00:02:40.808 --> 00:02:45.625
and another capital event every five
years, with every server to baseline,

45
00:02:45.625 --> 00:02:48.783
scan, patch and carry in
the system security plan.

46
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:53.364
Consolidate puts twenty-four distribution
points in twelve hubs for about ten

47
00:02:53.364 --> 00:02:55.586
million dollars in first-year capital,

48
00:02:55.586 --> 00:02:58.919
which also funds the transport
that cloud workloads need.

49
00:02:58.919 --> 00:03:04.220
Retire is the end state: no distribution
points at all, once five gates close.

50
00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.429
Consolidation is the interim step,

51
00:03:06.429 --> 00:03:11.130
so it fits an estate whose management
platform is already shrinking.

52
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.395
The target uses facilities
the agency already has:

53
00:03:18.395 --> 00:03:22.830
the primary data center, the recovery
site, and ten regional hubs.

54
00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:27.503
Two distribution points per hub gives
twenty-four, with capacity for ninety-six

55
00:03:27.503 --> 00:03:32.530
thousand clients, which is seventy-three
percent used at seventy thousand endpoints.

56
00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.416
Regional servers pull from
the two data centers,

57
00:03:35.416 --> 00:03:39.168
so the fan-out is two to twenty
instead of one to fifteen hundred.

58
00:03:39.168 --> 00:03:42.396
Each field office gets a
sized internet circuit,

59
00:03:42.396 --> 00:03:46.029
a five-G backup for continuity
only, and no local server.

60
00:03:46.029 --> 00:03:50.120
The design adds two servers per
eight thousand new endpoints.

61
00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:58.857
Without a branch server, three mechanisms
keep most bytes off the wide area network.

62
00:03:58.857 --> 00:04:02.049
Configuration Manager peer
cache lets designated

63
00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:04.945
clients share content
inside a boundary group.

64
00:04:04.945 --> 00:04:07.620
BranchCache does the same where enabled.

65
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:12.155
Delivery Optimization peers cloud
content, and for hub-and-spoke networks

66
00:04:12.155 --> 00:04:16.470
Microsoft recommends Group mode so
offices don't all share one address.

67
00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.707
All three are optimizations,
not dependencies.

68
00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:22.845
Connected Cache is the later
step for cloud content.

69
00:04:22.845 --> 00:04:27.158
In the government community cloud
the stand-alone form isn't excluded,

70
00:04:27.158 --> 00:04:30.132
but it needs its own host
and a boundary decision.

71
00:04:30.132 --> 00:04:32.860
Nothing before Phase three depends on it.

72
00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.949
The twenty-four distribution
point tier is interim,

73
00:04:39.949 --> 00:04:43.910
not a new floor, and five gates
decide when the last one retires.

74
00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.616
First, app content:
client apps on Intune and

75
00:04:46.616 --> 00:04:49.923
nothing still deployed from
Configuration Manager.

76
00:04:49.923 --> 00:04:54.523
Second, update content moved, and
the old update setting turned off.

77
00:04:54.523 --> 00:04:59.134
Third, imaging moved from task
sequences to Windows Autopilot.

78
00:04:59.134 --> 00:05:03.085
Fourth, no Connected Cache running
on distribution point hardware.

79
00:05:03.085 --> 00:05:07.035
Fifth, no compliance evidence
sourced from the site database.

80
00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:10.660
Close all five in month thirty
and retire in month thirty.

81
00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:12.310
Still open at month sixty?

82
00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:15.160
Keep the tier rather than force a cutover.

83
00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:21.960
First-year capital is
about ten million dollars.

84
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:26.153
Branch equipment is five million,
hub equipment about one million,

85
00:05:26.153 --> 00:05:29.660
and the control plane and
services nearly four million.

86
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:34.160
Steady-state recurring cost is about
fifteen point seven million a year,

87
00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.685
mostly internet circuits and
the security service edge.

88
00:05:37.685 --> 00:05:42.465
Against that, retiring the old circuits at
twelve hundred dollars an office a month

89
00:05:42.465 --> 00:05:44.613
removes twenty-one point six million,

90
00:05:44.613 --> 00:05:47.523
a net saving of about five
point nine million a year.

91
00:05:47.523 --> 00:05:52.611
These are planning estimates, not quotes,
with hardware at a forty percent discount.

92
00:05:52.611 --> 00:05:57.560
Replace every figure with the estate's
own data before a budget submission.

93
00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.093
Savings ramp as offices move
at circuit contract renewal,

94
00:06:05.093 --> 00:06:08.955
so year one is a net cost of ten
million and the program turns

95
00:06:08.955 --> 00:06:12.660
positive in year five, at about
three point three million.

96
00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:17.214
The refresh is minus fifty million in
year one and recurs at the next cycle,

97
00:06:17.214 --> 00:06:20.722
so the five-year difference
is about fifty-three million.

98
00:06:20.722 --> 00:06:25.622
One number decides the financial case:
current circuit cost per office per month.

99
00:06:25.622 --> 00:06:28.710
Break-even is eight hundred
seventy-three dollars.

100
00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.185
Above it, the program funds itself.

101
00:06:31.185 --> 00:06:33.968
Below it, it's still
cheaper than the refresh,

102
00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:36.960
but justify it on capability and mission.

103
00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:45.210
Phase zero, the first six months,
costs about one point eight million.

104
00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:50.511
It covers design, the security accreditation
package, the control plane, and a

105
00:06:50.511 --> 00:06:53.576
ten-site pilot, and it
exits on authorization,

106
00:06:53.576 --> 00:06:56.310
thirty stable days, and validated costs.

107
00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.585
Phase one builds the twelve hubs and
the twenty-four distribution points.

108
00:07:00.585 --> 00:07:05.891
Phase two moves offices in waves, timed to
circuit renewals so each wave helps fund

109
00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:11.198
the next, and no old circuit retires until
its replacement telemetry is confirmed.

110
00:07:11.198 --> 00:07:15.655
Phase three adds Microsoft
365 optimization and caching,

111
00:07:15.655 --> 00:07:18.998
and retires servers only
as the gates close.

112
00:07:18.998 --> 00:07:23.180
Accreditation, not procurement,
is the critical path.

113
00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.668
The implementation runs in ten stages,

114
00:07:29.668 --> 00:07:33.005
in order, and every stage
cites Microsoft Learn.

115
00:07:33.005 --> 00:07:38.433
Stage one analyzes the existing estate:
list every distribution point, its boundary

116
00:07:38.433 --> 00:07:43.705
groups and the clients it serves, and pull
circuit cost and quality for each office.

117
00:07:43.705 --> 00:07:47.442
Stage two sizes the result
against Microsoft's limits.

118
00:07:47.442 --> 00:07:51.266
Stage three is the funding
gate, the exit from Phase zero,

119
00:07:51.266 --> 00:07:54.342
where leadership funds
the hub tier or stops.

120
00:07:54.342 --> 00:07:57.827
Stage four builds the hubs
with pull distribution points,

121
00:07:57.827 --> 00:07:59.642
groups and prestaged content.

122
00:07:59.642 --> 00:08:01.868
Stage five turns on peer caching.

123
00:08:01.868 --> 00:08:06.230
The implementation guide and the
scripts kit are linked on the slide.

124
00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:15.005
Stage six moves offices in waves at
circuit renewal: each office's boundary

125
00:08:15.005 --> 00:08:20.355
joins its hub's boundary group, then the
office server goes read-only and retires.

126
00:08:20.355 --> 00:08:25.003
Stage seven packages applications
once with P S App Deploy Toolkit,

127
00:08:25.003 --> 00:08:28.655
so Configuration Manager
and Intune behave the same.

128
00:08:28.655 --> 00:08:32.070
Stage eight shifts co-management
workloads to Intune,

129
00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:34.043
starting with apps and updates.

130
00:08:34.043 --> 00:08:37.993
Stage nine adds Connected Cache
at the hubs for cloud content.

131
00:08:37.993 --> 00:08:43.742
Stage ten closes the five gates, and only
then retires the last distribution point.

132
00:08:43.742 --> 00:08:46.308
The guide and the scripts
kit are linked here too,

133
00:08:46.308 --> 00:08:48.460
for the people who will do the work.

134
00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:53.648
Three risks rate high.

135
00:08:53.648 --> 00:08:56.920
Accreditation slips: start
the package in Phase zero,

136
00:08:56.920 --> 00:09:01.159
and a twelve-month slip still leaves
the case better than the refresh,

137
00:09:01.159 --> 00:09:05.323
by about forty-seven million, though
break-even moves past year five.

138
00:09:05.323 --> 00:09:09.847
Circuit cost comes in below the
assumption: validate it before submission.

139
00:09:09.847 --> 00:09:12.629
And replacement telemetry lags a cutover:

140
00:09:12.629 --> 00:09:16.523
begin coordination in Phase zero
and gate each office on it.

141
00:09:16.523 --> 00:09:19.548
Ten assumptions in all
are ordered by impact.

142
00:09:19.548 --> 00:09:24.034
Check circuit cost, endpoint count
and terrestrial availability first.

143
00:09:24.034 --> 00:09:27.205
The architectural conclusion
holds across the range;

144
00:09:27.205 --> 00:09:30.380
the financial one depends
on the circuit number.

145
00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:38.843
There's a follow-on that makes consolidation
safer: standard application packaging.

146
00:09:38.843 --> 00:09:41.860
Intune Win32 apps must install silently,

147
00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:46.918
and Gate one only closes when the app
catalog moves, not just a setting.

148
00:09:46.918 --> 00:09:51.218
If each app is packaged twice,
the two paths behave differently.

149
00:09:51.218 --> 00:09:56.336
P S App Deploy Toolkit is a PowerShell
framework that standardizes deployment to

150
00:09:56.336 --> 00:10:01.380
Windows at scale, with first-class support
for Intune and Configuration Manager,

151
00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:04.542
and phases for before, during
and after the install.

152
00:10:04.542 --> 00:10:06.842
Package once, deliver from either.

153
00:10:06.842 --> 00:10:08.584
That's program design guidance,

154
00:10:08.584 --> 00:10:12.080
and it follows the decision
rather than conditioning it.

155
00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:16.980
Here is the ask.

156
00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:21.943
Defer the fifty million dollar refresh
pending a network modernization assessment.

157
00:10:21.943 --> 00:10:24.940
Have the sponsor state
whether it is acquisition

158
00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:27.280
or five-year cost, and itemize the scope.

159
00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.043
Validate the ten assumptions, starting
with circuit cost and endpoint count.

160
00:10:32.043 --> 00:10:36.230
Fund Phase zero, about one point
eight million over six months.

161
00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:39.893
Track the five gates, not a
date, for the last server.

162
00:10:39.893 --> 00:10:42.148
The owner is the sponsor with the CIO,

163
00:10:42.148 --> 00:10:45.380
and the timing is before
refresh money is committed.

164
00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.850
The decision guide and the
project plan carry the detail,

165
00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.890
and both are linked on this slide.
